  "title": "Melody M SOBE",
  "date": null,
  "content": "I was giving birth for the first time at age 21. This happened after a diagnosis of infertility and several years of praying for a baby. The pregnancy had been incredibly challenging, several instances of hemorrhaging from subchorionic bleeds and a 50/50 chance of survival for the baby earlier in the pregnancy. Birth was long, and much more physically difficult than my subsequent births have been. <br><br> When my body began spontaneously pushing, I experienced the worst pain of my life. I felt a sudden pulling at my chest. Then I felt as if I had left my body and was in the air hovering above and looking down at myself. I remember feeling neutral about this, and strongly thinking, 'I want to hold my baby.' <br><br> I entered my body again just as his head emerged. Then I pushed his shoulders out. I have never felt more blissful than when I was back in my body holding my newborn son. <br><br><span class=\"m108",
